# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. In production, set these in Vercel:
# Project Settings → Environment Variables. Never commit real values.
#
# Nothing here is needed to run the site locally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL                          required in production
#
# The site's origin — scheme and host, no path, no trailing slash. Canonical
# URLs, the sitemap, robots.txt and the social card are all built from it.
#
# A production build on Vercel FAILS without it, on purpose: there is no safe
# value to guess. Until a custom domain exists, use the project's production
# address, e.g. https://indie-portfolio.vercel.app, and change it when the
# domain arrives. It must be https in production. It is read at build time,
# so redeploy after changing it.
#
# Preview deployments fall back to their own generated address and are kept
# out of search engines. Locally it falls back to http://localhost:3000.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit counter — Redis                         required for a real count
#
# The count lives in Redis, because INCR is atomic on the server and every
# function instance sees the same number. Any Redis with an Upstash-style
# REST API works.
#
# On Vercel, add Upstash for Redis from the Marketplace (Storage tab) and
# connect it to this project. It injects these two, and nothing more is
# needed:
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=
#
# Or, with an Upstash database created directly, use Upstash's own names.
# If both pairs are set, these win.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=
#
# Without either pair:
#   - on Vercel, the counter is switched off and the page shows a dash (a
#     function has no persistent disk to count on), and the build warns;
#   - elsewhere, it writes to .data/visits.json — right for development and
#     for one self-hosted server with a persistent disk, wrong for anything
#     with more than one instance, and a production server says so in its
#     logs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VISITOR_COUNTER_KEY                           optional
#
# The Redis key holding the count. Defaults to portfolio:visits. Give preview
# deployments a different key if they share the production database, so
# previews do not add to the real count.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VISITOR_COUNTER_KEY=
